  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nSurface to mid level ridge will begin to break down on Wednesday \nbefore the arrival of the next system moving into the eastern Great \nLakes and Ohio Valley regions. Ahead of this, anticipate for mild \nand dry conditions to persist tonight into much of the day \nWednesday. Some rain showers may begin to arrive around sunset on \nWednesday evening ahead of the next system. Lows tonight will settle \nin the mid 30s, low 30s across inland Northwest Pennsylvania. Highs \ntomorrow rise into the mid to upper 50s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nAforementioned surface low and deepening upper trough will push east \ninto the region Wednesday evening. Rain showers will begin to spread \nwest to east across the area around sunset Wednesday with coverage \nincreasing Wednesday night into Thursday as better upper level \nsupport moves overhead. The surface low will exit to the east on \nThursday with the upper level trough persisting across the Great \nLakes region through the remainder of the week. By late Thursday \nnight/early Friday morning cooler air aloft, around -5 to -7C, will \nmove overhead and allow for precipitation to changeover to a \nrain/snow mix. Best potential for some wet snowflakes mixing in will \nremain across portions of Northeast Ohio and Northwest Pennsylvania \nearly Friday morning and again Friday night into Saturday. Impactful \naccumulations remain unlikely given warmer ground temperatures with \nthe best potential of measurable snow, albeit only a few tenths of \nan inch, across the higher terrain of Northwest Pennsylvania. \n\nAdditionally, northerly to northwesterly winds increase through the \nday on Thursday with wind gusts 25-30 MPH possible with localized \ngusts to 35 MPH closer to the lakeshore Thursday afternoon and \nevening. Northwesterly winds begin to decrease on Friday but it'll \nremain breezy with gusts to 20-25 MPH possible. \n\nA large area of high pressure will begin to build east into the \neastern CONUS late in the weekend and maintain control through early \nnext week. The high will start to dry out mid-levels Saturday into \nSunday, though light lake enhanced precipitation may continue into \nat least part of the weekend. High temperatures Thursday through the \nweekend will remain in the 40s with overnight lows falling to the \nmid 20s to low 30s.",
